<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Jumla</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1789 CE (approx.) · capital Jumla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading kingdom of the Karnali basin in western Nepal, heir to the Khasa Malla realm and head of the Baise Rajya confederation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khasa-malla-kingdom">Khasa Malla kingdom</a> broke up in the late 14th century, a Kalyal dynasty established itself in Jumla, the former Khasa heartland near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinja">Sinja</a>, around 1400. Its rulers claimed the prestige of the earlier Malla kings and became the most powerful of the hill states of the Karnali region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jumla was regarded as the senior state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baise-rajya">Baise Rajya</a>, the twenty-two kingdoms of western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>, and at its height received tribute from neighbouring rajas and from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mustang">Kingdom of Mustang</a>. Its economy rested on farming in high valleys and on the trade of salt and wool from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> for grain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khas-people">Khas</a> language marked its culture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> army of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a> conquered Jumla in 1789 after hard fighting, and its royal family fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>. Jumla became a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-jumla.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Jumla" width="320" height="179"><figcaption>Image: Priyankar Chand · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinja_Valley.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1789 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jumla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khas (Nepali)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jumla, Kalyal kingdom of Jumla</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jumla" aria-label="Kingdom of Jumla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6822 1107 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Jumla lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7142 1363L7131 1378M7142 1363L7239 1418M7142 1363L7189 1367"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7131 1378h0M7239 1418h0M7189 1367h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7142 1363h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7142 1363h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jumla. <a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jumla">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
